I apologize for the empty response earlier. Based on your original question, it seems like you wanted me to provide a Java code example that implements a tokenizer class and tests its functionality. Here's a complete example:

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

// Token interface
interface Token {
    String getValue();
}

// ParseException class
class ParseException extends Exception {}

// ExpressionConfiguration class
class ExpressionConfiguration {
    private String parseMethod;

    public ExpressionConfiguration(String parseMethod) {
        this.parseMethod = parseMethod;
    }

    public String getParseMethod() {
        return parseMethod;
    }
}

// Tokenizer class
public class Tokenizer {
    private ExpressionConfiguration configuration;

    public Tokenizer(ExpressionConfiguration configuration) {
        this.configuration = configuration;
    }

    // Method to tokenize the expression
    public List<Token> tokenize(String expression) throws ParseException {
        String[] tokens = expression.split("\\s+");
        List<Token> result = new ArrayList<>();

        for (String token : tokens) {
            if (token.matches("[a-zA-Z]+")) {
                Token tokenObj = new WordToken(token);
                result.add(tokenObj);
            } else if (token.matches("(\\d+)") || token.equals("(") || token.equals(")")) {
                Token tokenObj = new NumberToken(token);
                result.add(tokenObj);
            }
        }

        return result;
    }

    // Method to parse string literal
    public Token parseStringLiteral(String expression) throws ParseException {
        int startIndex = 0;
        int endIndex = expression.indexOf('"');

        if (endIndex == -1) {
            throw new ParseException("Invalid string literal");
        }

        String value = expression.substring(startIndex, endIndex + 1);
        return new StringToken(value);
    }
}

// Token classes
class WordToken implements Token {
    private String value;

    public WordToken(String value) {
        this.value = value;
    }

    @Override
    public String getValue() {
        return value;
    }
}

class NumberToken implements Token {
    private String value;

    public NumberToken(String value) {
        this.value = value;
    }

    @Override
    public String getValue() {
        return value;
    }
}

class StringToken implements Token {
    private String value;

    public StringToken(String value) {
        this.value = value;
    }

    @Override
    public String getValue() {
        return value;
    }
}

// Test class
public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) throws ParseException {
        ExpressionConfiguration config = new ExpressionConfiguration("token1");
        Tokenizer tokenizer = new Tokenizer(config);

        List<Token> tokens = tokenizer.tokenize("token1 + (2 * 3)");
        System.out.println(tokens);

        String tokenLiteral = "Hello World";
        Token stringToken = tokenizer.parseStringLiteral(tokenLiteral);
        System.out.println(stringToken.getValue());
    }
}
```

In this example, we have a `Tokenizer` class that takes an `ExpressionConfiguration` object in its constructor. The `tokenize()` method splits the input expression into tokens using regular expressions and returns them as a list. We also have a `parseStringLiteral()` method that extracts string literals from the input expression.

The `Main` class demonstrates how to use the `Tokenizer` class with an `ExpressionConfiguration` object. It creates an instance of `ExpressionConfiguration` and passes it to the `Tokenizer` constructor, then calls the `tokenize()` method to get the list of tokens for the expression "token1 + (2 * 3)". We also test the `parseStringLiteral()` method with a string literal.

Please note that this is a basic example and you may need to add more functionality to your tokenizer depending on your specific requirements.
